### ✅ **Implementation**

```java
import java.util.*;

/**
 * Represents an interval with start and end values.
 * @param <T> A comparable numeric type (e.g., Integer, Double).
 */
public record Interval<T extends Comparable<T>>(T start, T end) {}

/**
 * Merges overlapping intervals.
 * @param intervals A list of intervals to be merged.
 * @return A list of non-overlapping intervals sorted by start value.
 */
public class IntervalMerger {
    public static <T extends Comparable<T>> List<Interval<T>> mergeIntervals(List<Interval<T>> intervals) {
        if (intervals == null || intervals.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        // Sort intervals by start value
        intervals.sort(Comparator.comparing(Interval::start));

        List<Interval<T>> merged = new ArrayList<>();
        merged.add(intervals.get(0));

        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.size(); i++) {
            Interval<T> current = intervals.get(i);
            Interval<T> last = merged.get(merged.size() - 1);

            // Check if current interval overlaps with the last merged interval
            if (current.start.compareTo(last.end) <= 0) {
                // Merge intervals by updating the end of the last interval
                T newEnd = last.end.compareTo(current.end) > 0 ? last.end : current.end;
                merged.remove(merged.size() - 1);
                merged.add(new Interval<>(last.start, newEnd));
            } else {
                merged.add(current);
            }
        }

        return merged;
    }
}
```

---

### 🧪 **Comprehensive Test Cases**

